{"parentUuid":"e800194d-2a09-47e8-8d21-892a30c3276d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdvhoW1pyoUxTGNwX7Fs2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AP7h4NF73CaacrrFTUivfZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/zenray-gpt-realtime-audio/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":765,"cache_read_input_tokens":427263,"output_tokens":424,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":765,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":424,"cache_read_input_tokens":427263,"cache_creation_input_tokens":765,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":765},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdvhoFi6s8DFvgWD8Sb1v","type":"assistant","uuid":"fdf71969-8ac7-4e57-99f4-b379d885de78","timestamp":"2026-08-11T09:13:10.151Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"545b1129-5138-4cfb-81ca-0414eb8bfd2d","version":"2.1.222","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"fdf71969-8ac7-4e57-99f4-b379d885de78","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"69091160-6a2e-4eba-b42e-fc43199e0374","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AP7h4NF73CaacrrFTUivfZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ZenRay Dictate\n2\t\n3\tA tiny macOS menu bar app: **Fn** shows or hides a small ChatGPT window,\n4\t**Cmd+D** starts and stops dictation. When you stop, the transcript is\n5\talready on your clipboard. That's the whole app.\n6\t\n7\t![ZenRay Dictate mid-dictation](docs/screenshot.png)\n8\t\n9\t## Why\n10\t\n11\tI wanted ChatGPT's voice dictation — which I find noticeably more reliable\n12\tthan the local models used by some other dictation apps, especially in\n13\tFrench and Japanese — available system-wide with one key, without paying for\n14\ta separate transcription API.\n15\t\n16\tA caveat on that claim, so this doesn't read as more authoritative than it\n17\tis: I haven't found an independent benchmark of ChatGPT's own dictation\n18\tmodel to point to (it isn't listed on the couple of speech-to-text\n19\tleaderboards I checked). This is my own day-to-day experience, not a ranked\n20\tresult. In my use, local models like Parakeet frequently mis-detect French\n21\tspeech and fall back to English mid-sentence, and don't handle Japanese at\n22\tall, whereas ChatGPT's dictation does both. I've used it on both a ChatGPT\n23\tPlus account and a free account and haven't noticed a quality difference. It\n24\tcan be a couple of seconds slower to come back than a local model, which is\n25\ta trade I'll take for fewer wrong words.\n26\t\n27\t## How it works\n28\t\n29\tThe app keeps a hidden `chatgpt.com` session alive in a `WKWebView`. It does\n30\tnot reimplement ChatGPT's transcription request. It:\n31\t\n32\t1. Shows the page's own composer bar, trimmed down to just that bar (no\n33\t   sidebar, no chat history) — the black pill in the screenshot above is the\n34\t   real ChatGPT UI, not a custom recreation of it.\n35\t2. On Cmd+D, clicks whichever of ChatGPT's own `Start Dictation` /\n36\t   `Stop Dictation` buttons is currently showing (matched by an\n37\t   accessibility-label pattern, not a hardcoded string — those labels have\n38\t   changed between ChatGPT builds during development).\n39\t3. Watches the response of `POST /backend-api/transcribe`\n40\t   (`{\"text\": ..., \"asset_format\": \"webm\"}`) and copies the text to the\n41\t   clipboard.\n42\t\n43\tNo keystrokes are simulated into the page, no request is rebuilt by hand.\n44\t\n45\t## A note on Terms of Service\n46\t\n47\tThis automates a real, logged-in ChatGPT web session rather than going\n48\tthrough OpenAI's official API, which is outside what OpenAI's Terms of\n49\tService cover for automated access. It only ever touches your own account —\n50\tthere's no bypassing payment, no scraping anyone else's data — but the risk\n51\tof a flag or restriction on your account is real and it's yours to carry if\n52\tyou use this. I'm saying this plainly rather than dressing it up, so you can\n53\tdecide with the same information I had.\n54\t\n55\t## Setup\n56\t\n57\tRequires macOS 14+ and a ChatGPT account (Plus or free).\n58\t\n59\t```bash\n60\tgit clone https://github.com/matthieuhuguet/zenray-dictate.git\n61\tcd zenray-dictate\n62\t./make-certificate.sh   # once: a local code-signing identity\n63\t./build.sh\n64\topen ZenRayDictate.app\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t**Why the certificate script.** An ad-hoc signature changes on every build,\n68\tand macOS ties the Accessibility permission to that exact signature — so\n69\twithout a stable identity, Accessibility silently revokes itself on every\n70\trebuild even though the tick in System Settings never changes. Not needed\n71\tagain after the first run; `build.sh` reuses the same identity from then on.\n72\t\n73\t**First run.**\n74\t\n75\t1. The window opens on its own. Sign in to ChatGPT normally. The session\n76\t   persists across future launches.\n77\t2. Grant the microphone when asked. If Fn does nothing outside the app,\n78\t   grant Accessibility too (System Settings → Privacy & Security →\n79\t   Accessibility).\n80\t3. System Settings → Keyboard → \"Press 🌐 key to\" → **Do Nothing**. Without\n81\t   this, macOS intercepts Fn for its own emoji picker or dictation before the\n82\t   app ever sees it.\n83\t\n84\t**Using it.** Cmd+D anywhere: the bar appears, dictation starts. Talk. Cmd+D\n85\tagain: it stops, and the text is on your clipboard — `Cmd+V` to paste. Fn\n86\tshows or hides the window on its own, independent of dictation.\n87\t\n88\t## Known limitations, help wanted\n89\t\n90\t- **Fn doesn't reliably bring the window back once it's lost focus** — click\n91\t  another app, and pressing Fn again sometimes does nothing; you have to\n92\t  click the app's Dock icon instead. I'd genuinely like help tracking this\n93\t  down — issues and PRs welcome.\n94\t- Cmd+D is a system-wide shortcut while this app is running, so it stops\n95\t  reaching Cmd+D in other apps (Safari's bookmark shortcut, Finder's\n96\t  Duplicate) for as long as ZenRay Dictate is open.\n97\t- Dictation opens in a temporary ChatGPT chat, so nothing is saved to your\n98\t  ChatGPT history.\n99\t- If ChatGPT changes its accessibility labels again, the button matching in\n100\t  `bridge.js` may need a small update.\n101\t\n102\t## Compared to\n103\t\n104\t| | Cost | Model |\n105\t|---|---|---|\n106\t| **ZenRay Dictate** | Free (uses your ChatGPT account) | ChatGPT's dictation, cloud |\n107\t| [SuperWhisper](https://superwhisper.com) | Free tier + Pro ($8.49/mo or a one-time lifetime price) | Local + cloud models |\n108\t| [Handy](https://github.com/cjpais/Handy) | Free, open source (MIT) | Fully offline, local models |\n109\t\n110\tHandy in particular is free and open source, contrary to what I first\n111\tassumed when writing this — worth a look if you'd rather everything stay\n112\tlocal and never touch a ChatGPT account.\n113\t\n114\t## Project layout\n115\t\n116\t| File | Role |\n117\t|---|---|\n118\t| `main.swift` | Entry point, regular app so it also gets a Dock icon |\n119\t| `AppDelegate.swift` | Menu bar, permissions at launch, wires Cmd+D and Fn |\n120\t| `ChatWindow.swift` | The window: compact composer bar, sign-in mode, JS bridge, clipboard |\n121\t| `GlobalHotKey.swift` | Cmd+D via `RegisterEventHotKey` — no permission required |\n122\t| `FnKeyMonitor.swift` | Fn via a listen-only `CGEventTap` — needs Accessibility |\n123\t| `LoginItem.swift` | Launch at login, via `SMAppService` |\n124\t| `Permissions.swift` | Microphone and Accessibility helpers |\n125\t| `Log.swift` | Plain text log at `~/Library/Logs/ZenRayDictate.log` |\n126\t| `Resources/bridge.js` | Everything that touches the ChatGPT page itself |\n127\t| `Entitlements.plist` | Declares microphone access under the hardened runtime |\n128\t| `make-certificate.sh` | One-time local signing identity, see Setup |\n129\t\n130\t## License\n131\t\n132\tMIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n133\t"}]},"uuid":"07fb23e9-a1db-48d2-81b0-4aa2d9f3bf62","timestamp":"2026-08-11T09:13:10.155Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Productions/GithubProductions/zenray-gpt-realtime-audio/README.md","content":"# ZenRay Dictate\n\nA tiny macOS menu bar app: **Fn** shows or hides a small ChatGPT window,\n**Cmd+D** starts and stops dictation. When you stop, the transcript is\nalready on your clipboard. That's the whole app.\n\n![ZenRay Dictate mid-dictation](docs/screenshot.png)\n\n## Why\n\nI wanted ChatGPT's voice dictation — which I find noticeably more reliable\nthan the local models used by some other dictation apps, especially in\nFrench and Japanese — available system-wide with one key, without paying for\na separate transcription API.\n\nA caveat on that claim, so this doesn't read as more authoritative than it\nis: I haven't found an independent benchmark of ChatGPT's own dictation\nmodel to point to (it isn't listed on the couple of speech-to-text\nleaderboards I checked). This is my own day-to-day experience, not a ranked\nresult. In my use, local models like Parakeet frequently mis-detect French\nspeech and fall back to English mid-sentence, and don't handle Japanese at\nall, whereas ChatGPT's dictation does both. I've used it on both a ChatGPT\nPlus account and a free account and haven't noticed a quality difference. It\ncan be a couple of seconds slower to come back than a local model, which is\na trade I'll take for fewer wrong words.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe app keeps a hidden `chatgpt.com` session alive in a `WKWebView`. It does\nnot reimplement ChatGPT's transcription request. It:\n\n1. Shows the page's own composer bar, trimmed down to just that bar (no\n   sidebar, no chat history) — the black pill in the screenshot above is the\n   real ChatGPT UI, not a custom recreation of it.\n2. On Cmd+D, clicks whichever of ChatGPT's own `Start Dictation` /\n   `Stop Dictation` buttons is currently showing (matched by an\n   accessibility-label pattern, not a hardcoded string — those labels have\n   changed between ChatGPT builds during development).\n3. Watches the response of `POST /backend-api/transcribe`\n   (`{\"text\": ..., \"asset_format\": \"webm\"}`) and copies the text to the\n   clipboard.\n\nNo keystrokes are simulated into the page, no request is rebuilt by hand.\n\n## A note on Terms of Service\n\nThis automates a real, logged-in ChatGPT web session rather than going\nthrough OpenAI's official API, which is outside what OpenAI's Terms of\nService cover for automated access. It only ever touches your own account —\nthere's no bypassing payment, no scraping anyone else's data — but the risk\nof a flag or restriction on your account is real and it's yours to carry if\nyou use this. I'm saying this plainly rather than dressing it up, so you can\ndecide with the same information I had.\n\n## Setup\n\nRequires macOS 14+ and a ChatGPT account (Plus or free).\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/matthieuhuguet/zenray-dictate.git\ncd zenray-dictate\n./make-certificate.sh   # once: a local code-signing identity\n./build.sh\nopen ZenRayDictate.app\n```\n\n**Why the certificate script.** An ad-hoc signature changes on every build,\nand macOS ties the Accessibility permission to that exact signature — so\nwithout a stable identity, Accessibility silently revokes itself on every\nrebuild even though the tick in System Settings never changes. Not needed\nagain after the first run; `build.sh` reuses the same identity from then on.\n\n**First run.**\n\n1. The window opens on its own. Sign in to ChatGPT normally. The session\n   persists across future launches.\n2. Grant the microphone when asked. If Fn does nothing outside the app,\n   grant Accessibility too (System Settings → Privacy & Security →\n   Accessibility).\n3. System Settings → Keyboard → \"Press 🌐 key to\" → **Do Nothing**. Without\n   this, macOS intercepts Fn for its own emoji picker or dictation before the\n   app ever sees it.\n\n**Using it.** Cmd+D anywhere: the bar appears, dictation starts. Talk. Cmd+D\nagain: it stops, and the text is on your clipboard — `Cmd+V` to paste. Fn\nshows or hides the window on its own, independent of dictation.\n\n## Known limitations, help wanted\n\n- **Fn doesn't reliably bring the window back once it's lost focus** — click\n  another app, and pressing Fn again sometimes does nothing; you have to\n  click the app's Dock icon instead. I'd genuinely like help tracking this\n  down — issues and PRs welcome.\n- Cmd+D is a system-wide shortcut while this app is running, so it stops\n  reaching Cmd+D in other apps (Safari's bookmark shortcut, Finder's\n  Duplicate) for as long as ZenRay Dictate is open.\n- Dictation opens in a temporary ChatGPT chat, so nothing is saved to your\n  ChatGPT history.\n- If ChatGPT changes its accessibility labels again, the button matching in\n  `bridge.js` may need a small update.\n\n## Compared to\n\n| | Cost | Model |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ZenRay Dictate** | Free (uses your ChatGPT account) | ChatGPT's dictation, cloud |\n| [SuperWhisper](https://superwhisper.com) | Free tier + Pro ($8.49/mo or a one-time lifetime price) | Local + cloud models |\n| [Handy](https://github.com/cjpais/Handy) | Free, open source (MIT) | Fully offline, local models |\n\nHandy in particular is free and open source, contrary to what I first\nassumed when writing this — worth a look if you'd rather everything stay\nlocal and never touch a ChatGPT account.\n\n## Project layout\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `main.swift` | Entry point, regular app so it also gets a Dock icon |\n| `AppDelegate.swift` | Menu bar, permissions at launch, wires Cmd+D and Fn |\n| `ChatWindow.swift` | The window: compact composer bar, sign-in mode, JS bridge, clipboard |\n| `GlobalHotKey.swift` | Cmd+D via `RegisterEventHotKey` — no permission required |\n| `FnKeyMonitor.swift` | Fn via a listen-only `CGEventTap` — needs Accessibility |\n| `LoginItem.swift` | Launch at login, via `SMAppService` |\n| `Permissions.swift` | Microphone and Accessibility helpers |\n| `Log.swift` | Plain text log at `~/Library/Logs/ZenRayDictate.log` |\n| `Resources/bridge.js` | Everything that touches the ChatGPT page itself |\n| `Entitlements.plist` | Declares microphone access under the hardened runtime |\n| `make-certificate.sh` | One-time local signing identity, see Setup |\n\n## License\n\nMIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n","numLines":133,"startLine":1,"totalLines":133}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fdf71969-8ac7-4e57-99f4-b379d885de78","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"545b1129-5138-4cfb-81ca-0414eb8bfd2d","version":"2.1.222","gitBranch":"main"}
